WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:05.679
All right, let's begin with an overview of ICV effects.

2
00:00:05.679 --> 00:00:07.900
So when we talk about ICV effects,

3
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.679
it actually falls under an umbrella term

4
00:00:10.679 --> 00:00:12.179
called virtual production.

5
00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:14.080
And virtual production has a number

6
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.199
of different classifications that we should discuss.

7
00:00:17.199 --> 00:00:19.699
Number one, visualization.

8
00:00:19.699 --> 00:00:23.140
This is basically what we originally considered

9
00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.660
pre-visualization, and it includes a number

10
00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.620
of various forms of 3D pre-visualization

11
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.219
and virtual scouting.

12
00:00:30.219 --> 00:00:33.060
A lot of these tools have basically evolved

13
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.420
to be part of this process,

14
00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.780
and they're included under the entire term

15
00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.570
of virtual production.

16
00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.189
Secondly, there's performance capture,

17
00:00:41.189 --> 00:00:43.210
and this again, another broad term,

18
00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:46.490
it includes things like standard mocap,

19
00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:48.750
performance capture in general,

20
00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.210
facial capture, and even keyframe animation.

21
00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:55.490
Everything needs to have some sort of source

22
00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:57.460
for performance.

23
00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.820
Then we move on to traditional hybridized

24
00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:00.979
VFX production.

25
00:01:00.979 --> 00:01:04.280
Now this is getting into a much larger space.

26
00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.420
This is the classic green screen

27
00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:09.640
along with tracking markers that is used

28
00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:14.640
to construct various VFX shots,

29
00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.980
typically building out various layers

30
00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.980
and bringing things together in comp

31
00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:23.299
at a post-production facility.

32
00:01:23.299 --> 00:01:26.659
And then finally, we have in-camera visual effects.

33
00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:29.180
And it's this particular category

34
00:01:29.379 --> 00:01:30.579
that we want to talk about today.

35
00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:32.900
This is the whole LED wall,

36
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.159
virtual production-based process

37
00:01:35.159 --> 00:01:38.700
in which you're filming everything through the camera

38
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.180
along with what you're seeing behind

39
00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:44.959
the actual foreground actor on an LED wall.

40
00:01:44.959 --> 00:01:46.739
But there are a number of different project types

41
00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:48.340
that we usually refer to,

42
00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:51.819
and they are basically fully animated production.

43
00:01:51.819 --> 00:01:53.379
This is a form of virtual production.

44
00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:57.140
Obviously everything is computer generated and in CG.

45
00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.459
There's a live action equivalent, of course,

46
00:01:59.459 --> 00:02:02.260
where we're shooting live action content

47
00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:05.420
and we're using the virtual production

48
00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:10.419
and VFX process in order to comp in various backgrounds,

49
00:02:10.939 --> 00:02:14.340
matte extensions and set extensions

50
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:17.180
and other types of effects.

51
00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:20.580
And then finally, the whole CG live action hybrid,

52
00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:24.099
we're bringing it all together on the stage

53
00:02:24.099 --> 00:02:26.879
in-camera visual effects in hand.

54
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.479
In order to get a better appreciation

55
00:02:29.479 --> 00:02:32.780
as to how in-camera visual effects developed,

56
00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.719
we're gonna look at some historical context.

57
00:02:35.719 --> 00:02:38.719
And this is always something that's of interest

58
00:02:38.719 --> 00:02:40.159
to a lot of people,

59
00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:45.159
but ICV effects just didn't spontaneously appear.

60
00:02:45.159 --> 00:02:49.960
It actually has a number of different forerunners to it.

61
00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:54.259
So let's first talk about the Pepper's ghost technique.

62
00:02:54.259 --> 00:02:57.219
This is basically, it's an optical illusion.

63
00:02:57.219 --> 00:03:02.139
It was designed to reflect a performance on a pane of glass

64
00:03:02.139 --> 00:03:05.180
that was between the performer and the actual audience.

65
00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:07.419
And this was popularized by Disney

66
00:03:07.419 --> 00:03:09.539
for its haunted mansion ride.

67
00:03:09.539 --> 00:03:14.460
And it's actually been used today at current modern times,

68
00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:17.659
particularly for producing like a form of hologram

69
00:03:17.659 --> 00:03:21.180
of famous actors like Michael Jackson or Tupac.

70
00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.740
There was a number of them that have used this technique.

71
00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.620
Next, we'll talk about rear screen projection.

72
00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:28.419
Now, this is kind of a classic,

73
00:03:28.419 --> 00:03:30.939
and it's usually associated with a lot of people

74
00:03:30.939 --> 00:03:35.379
who consider these films to be like B movies nowadays,

75
00:03:35.379 --> 00:03:38.879
but this was really a simple technique.

76
00:03:38.879 --> 00:03:41.259
It was basically combining a projected

77
00:03:41.259 --> 00:03:43.560
pre-filmed reversed background,

78
00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.699
and it was projected onto a screen

79
00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:50.340
so that you'd have a central foreground actor

80
00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.259
and the rear screen behind him.

81
00:03:53.259 --> 00:03:57.020
This technique was perfected pretty much,

82
00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:00.139
its highest point was 2001, A Space Odyssey.

83
00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:01.500
They used it there,

84
00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.900
but it was used on an array of different films

85
00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:08.180
throughout the 1930s, all the way up to the 1970s.

86
00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:10.699
Next, we have front screen projection.

87
00:04:10.699 --> 00:04:12.259
Now, this is basically,

88
00:04:12.259 --> 00:04:14.860
it's a derivative of rear screen projection,

89
00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:17.579
where again, that pre-filmed content

90
00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:20.779
was composited behind the actor,

91
00:04:20.779 --> 00:04:24.939
but the way that the image was brought and projected

92
00:04:24.939 --> 00:04:25.939
was a little bit of a trick.

93
00:04:25.939 --> 00:04:27.939
They usually typically use like a prism

94
00:04:27.939 --> 00:04:29.540
or some sort of beam splitter

95
00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:33.579
in order to project the image

96
00:04:33.579 --> 00:04:34.980
from the front of the screen

97
00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:36.740
rather than from the back of the screen.

98
00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:40.740
And this afforded us some extra benefits.

99
00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.100
Number one, the image was brighter

100
00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:46.379
and the colors were more saturated.

101
00:04:46.379 --> 00:04:49.740
And this basically is, again,

102
00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:50.980
still being used today.

103
00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:52.740
There's been a number of modern productions

104
00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:53.579
that have used this,

105
00:04:53.579 --> 00:04:55.939
like Oblivion used front screen projection

106
00:04:55.939 --> 00:04:58.939
for a number of its visual effects shots,

107
00:04:58.939 --> 00:05:01.620
but it's films that you normally saw

108
00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:04.540
during the 80s, 90s and early 2000s.

109
00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:09.300
Let's take a look at our next one, which was dynamation.

110
00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.699
Now, this is a Ray Harryhausen technique

111
00:05:11.699 --> 00:05:14.100
that is a combination of different things.

112
00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:16.899
It's basically combining stop motion animation

113
00:05:16.939 --> 00:05:18.540
on one plane,

114
00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:22.139
along with a rear projected screen on another plane,

115
00:05:22.139 --> 00:05:25.300
and then a foreground matte.

116
00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:29.220
And this is kind of a very interesting precursor

117
00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:30.740
to a number of different techniques

118
00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:32.459
used in animation as well.

119
00:05:32.459 --> 00:05:34.420
I list it within virtual production

120
00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.660
as a forerunner for all of this

121
00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:39.100
because it is really forward-thinking

122
00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.699
in a lot of different ways.

123
00:05:40.699 --> 00:05:43.779
Next is the sodium vapor process.

124
00:05:43.779 --> 00:05:46.139
Now, this process utilizes

125
00:05:46.139 --> 00:05:49.379
a photochemical approach to filmmaking,

126
00:05:49.379 --> 00:05:53.339
where it combines actors and backgrounds together.

127
00:05:53.339 --> 00:05:55.540
This originated in the 1950s,

128
00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:58.740
and it was heavily utilized by Walt Disney again,

129
00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:01.060
and they called it yellow screening.

130
00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:03.540
And this utilizes basically really powerful

131
00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:05.779
sodium vapor lights

132
00:06:05.779 --> 00:06:09.220
that have a specific wavelength and cast,

133
00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:13.980
and a camera that, again, utilizing a beam splitter,

134
00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:17.259
this prism exposed two different,

135
00:06:17.259 --> 00:06:20.019
basically separate films.

136
00:06:20.019 --> 00:06:24.100
And one would be the beauty pass in full color,

137
00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:27.660
and the other would basically be a black and white matte pass

138
00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:32.319
that was used to help with bringing the composite together.

139
00:06:32.319 --> 00:06:34.519
This was the first precursor

140
00:06:34.519 --> 00:06:36.399
to the blue screening techniques,

141
00:06:36.399 --> 00:06:39.160
which we see next, blue chroma key.

142
00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:42.439
This was designed after the traveling matte technique

143
00:06:42.439 --> 00:06:45.560
was perfected and developed by RKO Pictures,

144
00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:48.240
Radio Pictures in the 1930s.

145
00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:51.360
Blue screen process developed along yellow screening

146
00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:53.199
and used a very similar technique

147
00:06:53.199 --> 00:06:56.040
of combining an optical printer with two projectors

148
00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:58.399
and a camera and a beam splitter

149
00:06:58.399 --> 00:07:00.959
in order to produce these two passes,

150
00:07:00.959 --> 00:07:04.079
one for the beauty pass and one for the matte pass.

151
00:07:04.079 --> 00:07:05.519
This blue screening technique

152
00:07:05.519 --> 00:07:08.480
eventually became the favorite over yellow screening,

153
00:07:08.519 --> 00:07:11.639
and it was used a lot during the early,

154
00:07:11.639 --> 00:07:14.220
or excuse me, the late 20th century.

155
00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:17.699
Blue screening eventually evolved into green screening,

156
00:07:17.699 --> 00:07:19.459
and this is a process that was developed

157
00:07:19.459 --> 00:07:23.540
in the early 1970s by American British television networks

158
00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:25.899
and various newscast centers.

159
00:07:25.899 --> 00:07:27.339
We were starting to see the beginnings

160
00:07:27.339 --> 00:07:29.019
of the microcomputer age,

161
00:07:29.019 --> 00:07:33.459
and this basically assisted and eventually evolved

162
00:07:33.459 --> 00:07:35.899
past the optical printing process

163
00:07:35.899 --> 00:07:38.579
and started to combine, of course,

164
00:07:38.579 --> 00:07:41.860
with computer graphic imagery.

165
00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:45.139
So green was selected over blue

166
00:07:45.139 --> 00:07:47.860
just due to the fact that green was not

167
00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:51.379
as heavily utilized and is not normally found

168
00:07:51.379 --> 00:07:55.100
in typically in the skin of most people.

169
00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:58.060
Green screen was just a better approach for that,

170
00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:01.300
whereas you see that a lot of skin tones

171
00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:03.300
have a lot of blue in it.

172
00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:06.779
So they moved over to green as a better process.

173
00:08:06.779 --> 00:08:09.180
Ultimately, through the use and advancements

174
00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:12.620
of computer graphic and aided imagery,

175
00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:15.339
the green screen technique was combined

176
00:08:15.339 --> 00:08:19.579
with tracking markers where the movement of the camera

177
00:08:19.579 --> 00:08:21.100
could be reverse engineered

178
00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:24.579
and fed into various different compositing programs,

179
00:08:24.579 --> 00:08:28.980
allowing you to basically combine computer graphic animation

180
00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:32.549
along with live action film.

181
00:08:32.549 --> 00:08:35.149
This ultimately gave way and is giving way

182
00:08:35.149 --> 00:08:37.230
to the process that we're talking about today,

183
00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:40.070
which is in-camera visual effects.

184
00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:44.350
Now, this is basically the earliest forerunner to this.

185
00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:45.590
You're probably familiar with it.

186
00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:47.309
It's the Mandalorian.

187
00:08:47.309 --> 00:08:51.909
And this was basically a whole amalgamation of techniques

188
00:08:51.909 --> 00:08:53.230
that are being brought together.

189
00:08:53.230 --> 00:08:56.789
Computer graphic technologies, motion capture,

190
00:08:56.789 --> 00:09:00.029
camera tracking, all of this is combined

191
00:09:00.029 --> 00:09:04.309
with live real-time graphics that are being generated

192
00:09:04.309 --> 00:09:06.629
and put onto an LED wall.

193
00:09:06.629 --> 00:09:08.429
And this is something that we're gonna be talking

194
00:09:08.429 --> 00:09:11.470
about today, what does this LED wall look like,

195
00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:14.350
how it's put together and constructed and so forth.

196
00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:16.350
And this allows, of course,

197
00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:18.909
for any kind of computer-generated imagery

198
00:09:18.909 --> 00:09:20.870
to be placed behind the actor.

199
00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:23.509
And when combined with some mid-ground elements

200
00:09:23.509 --> 00:09:25.389
and some foreground action,

201
00:09:25.389 --> 00:09:30.389
you can construct some very, very believable effects

202
00:09:30.669 --> 00:09:33.710
that can be shot in camera in real time.

203
00:09:33.710 --> 00:09:36.710
And it's really quite an advancement.
